## Hole mobility properties and morphologies
Hole mobility is an i mporta nt fa ctor f or the perf orma nce of PSCs. The hole mobilities of PDTPO-IDT and PDTPO-IDTT in both pure films and blend films were investigated by employing hole-only devices with the following structure:
$170/\mathsf{M o O}_{3}/\mathsf{p o l y m e r}/\mathsf{M o O}_{3}/\mathsf{A l}$ f or the pure fil ms a nd $\mathsf{I T O/M o O}_{3}/\mathsf{p o l}\mathsf{y m e r};\mathsf{P C}_{71}\mathsf{B M}/\mathsf{M o O}_{3}/\mathsf{A l}$ f or the bl end fil ms usi ng the space- charge-limited current (SCLC) method. Currentvoltage $(I-V)$ cha ra cteristi cs a nd SCLC fitti ngs of devices are shown in Fig. 5. In the pure films, PDTPO-IDT and PDTPO-IDTT exhi bit the sa me hol e mobiliti es of $1.0\times10^{-3}\mathsf{c m}^{2}\mathsf{V}^{-1}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ . I n t he blend films without DIO, the hole mobilities of PDTPO-IDT and PDTPO-IDTT are $7.6\times10^{-4}\ c m^{2}\vee^{-1}\mathsf s^{-1}$ and $6.0{\times}10^{-4}\ {\mathsf c m}^{2}\ {\mathsf V}^{-1}\ {\mathsf s}^{-1},$ respectively. When the $3\%$ DIO was added, the hole mobilities are increas ed to $8.7\times10^{-4}\ c m^{2}\vee^{-1}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ and $7.6\times10^{-4}\mathsf{c m}^{2}\mathsf{V}^{-1}\mathsf{s}^{-1},$ , res pectively. Compared with the pure films, the presence of $\mathsf{P C}_{71}\mathsf{B M}$ in blend films had a negative eff ect on the molecular packing and the hole mobility. But the difference of hole mobilities between pure film and blend film are not obvious, indicating that the two polymers have a good miscibility with $\mathsf{P C}_{71}\mathsf{B M}$ under the aid of solution annealing by molecular reorganization and repacking, which will contribute to the formation of good phase separations and favorable morphologies. This can also be evident from the atomic force micros copy (AFM) images s hown in Fig. 6. The pure films of PDTPO-IDT and PDTPO-IDTT displayed smooth and uniform morphology with root-mean-square (RMS) surface roughness of $0.875\ \mathsf{n m}$ and $0.986\ \mathsf{n m}$ , res pectively. When $\mathsf{P C}_{71}\mathsf{B M}$ was blended without $3\%$ DIO a dditi ve, it is clea rl y obs erved f rom AFM images (Fig. 6b and 6e) that many small particles aggregated to form severe phase separations, which is not conducive to charge carriers trans port. When $3\%$ DIO was added, the small particles disappeared and the surface morphology returned to more smooth and uniform state with RMS roughness of $0.679\ \mathsf{n m}$ and $0.719\ \mathrm{~nm~}$ , res pecti v el y, indicating that DIO addition played an important role in morphology controlling.
PDTPO-IDT and PDTPO-IDTT possess deeper HOMO energy levels than that of P3HT (-5.19 eV) by about $0.23\ \mathrm{eV}$ . Hence, higher $V_{\circ\mathsf{c}}s$ of PDTPO-IDT and PDTPO-IDTT based PSCs could be anticipated compared to that of P3HT based PSC. Because the $V_{\mathsf{o c}}$ value and the difference in energy between the HOMO of electron donor and the LUMO of electron acceptor are positively linear correlation. 50,51 Interestingl y, the HOMO
energy level of PDTPO-IDT is higher than that of PDTPO-BDTO $(-5.38\ \mathsf{e V})$ by $0.06\ \mathrm{eV},^{40}$ however, the PDTPO-IDT based PSC achieved a higher $V_{\mathsf{o c}}$ (Table 3). This cannot be simply explained by using a bove-mentioned principle. The more accurate Voc can be expressed as following equation:46
$$
V_{o c}=\frac{E_{\mathrm{LUMO}}^{\mathrm{A}}-E_{\mathrm{HOMO}}^{\mathrm{D}}}{q}-\frac{k T}{q}\times\ln\frac{N_{\mathrm{LUMO}}^{\mathrm{A}}N_{\mathrm{HOMO}}^{\mathrm{D}}}{n p}
$$
Where $E_{\mathsf{L U M O}}^{\mathsf{A}}$ is the LUMO energy level of acceptor (A) and $\bar{E}_{\mathsf{H O M O}}^{\mathsf{D}}$ is the HOMO energy level of donor (D). $N_{\mathsf{L U M O}}^{\mathsf{A}}$ is the density of states of the LUMO of the acceptor and $N_{{\mathsf{H O M O}}}^{\mathsf{D}}$ is the density of sta tes of the HOMO of the donor. $n$ is the density of electrons of acceptor and $p$ is the density of holes of donor. $q$ is the elementary charge, $k$ is the Boltz ma nn’s consta nt, a nd $\tau$ is the absolute temperature. After replacing BDTO with laddertype IDT, the hole mobility of blend film of PDTPO-IDT is nearly sevenfold higher than that of PDTPO-BDTO $\left(1.25\times10^{-4}\ c m^{2}\ V^{-1}\right)$ $\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ ), which means that larger hole and electron concentration gra dients woul d be built up i n the bl end fil m, foll owi ng tha t the values of $n$ and $p$ accordingly increase. Meanwhile, a nearly same $N_{\mathsf{L U M O}}^{\mathsf{A}}N_{\mathsf{H O M O}}^{\mathsf{D}}$ can be predicted in two PSCs , indicated by approximately equivalent $J_{\mathsf{s c}}$ of two PSCs. A l ower loss of $V_{\mathsf{o c}}$ can be calculated from the second term of equation 1 for PSC with PDTPO-IDT. According to the equation, although the first term of equa tion 1 is somewha t smaller for $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ of PDTPO-IDT based PSC, it can still yield a higher $V_{\circ\mathsf{c}}$ over PDTPO-BDTO based PSC.
## Conclusion
In summary, we developed two new wide bandgap polymers by the combination of the ladder-type IDT/IDTT units with well-planarity DTPO unit. Both of the two polymers showed wide optical bandgaps exceeding 2.0 eV and deep HOMO energy levels of ca. -5.32 eV. PSCs based on PDTPO-IDT with bandgap up to $2.05\mathrm{eV}$ achieved a remarkable $V_{\mathsf{o c}}$ of 0.97 V and a high efficiency of $7.33\%$ . PDTPO-IDTT based PSC showed a moderate PCE of $5.47\%$ . The s mall torsion angle, high hole mobility and uniform morphology contribute to the high performance of the PDTPO-IDT based PSCs. We believe that the wide optical bandgap and excellent device performance will make PDTPO-IDT as a promising candidate for tandem and ternary organic solar cells.
